# ViGov — environment template.
#
# PLACEHOLDERS ONLY. A real value here reaches git the moment someone forgets, and a secret in
# git cannot be recalled: the history is already on every clone. Where one process serves 200+
# communes, one leaked signing key affects every commune, not one (rule 8).
#
#   cp .env.example .env     then fill .env, which .gitignore already blocks
#
# ONE FILE FOR ALL EIGHT SERVICES, on purpose. pkg/config.Load(serviceName) reads the same
# platform-wide constants everywhere, so a template per service would be eight copies of one
# fact — and the copy nobody updates is the one the next person follows (rule 9).
#
# WHAT DOES NOT BELONG HERE: anything that differs per commune — name, upper authority, logo,
# coordinates, SLA figures, working hours, public holidays, duty phone. Those are read AT
# RUNTIME from per-tenant configuration in the database. An environment variable is a constant
# of the process, and one process serves every commune (rule 1, invariant 10).
#
# Each variable is documented where it is read: pkg/config/config.go.

# ---- required ----------------------------------------------------------------

# This service's OWN schema. Never another service's (rule 2, forbidden #2).
DATABASE_DSN=postgres://<user>:<password>@<host>:5432/<database>?sslmode=require

# dev | staging | prod. Decides nothing about business behaviour — only how strict startup is.
ENV=dev

# Signing keys for the session token, newest FIRST.
#
# A LIST, not one key: the first signs, every entry still verifies. That is what makes rotation
# possible — with a single key, rotating it signs every open session out, across every commune
# at once (rule 8, invariant 6). Rotate by prepending the new key, then dropping the last one a
# session lifetime later.
#
#   generate:  openssl rand -base64 32
#
SESSION_SIGNING_KEYS=<new-key>,<previous-key>

# ---- optional ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Duplicate-request protection and rate limiting. Empty outside dev is reported at every
# startup: with no Redis there is nothing standing between a double-submitted POST and a second
# permanent record.
REDIS_DSN=redis://<host>:6379/0

# Default :8080
LISTEN_ADDR=

# Inter-service gRPC. Default :9090 — a SEPARATE port from LISTEN_ADDR because gRPC needs
# HTTP/2 while the REST surface is served to browsers over HTTP/1.1. This port is internal:
# it belongs behind the cluster boundary and must not be published to the internet.
GRPC_LISTEN_ADDR=

# ---- required, every service, every environment ---------------------------------

# The caller key for the inter-service gRPC port. ONE value, the SAME value in every service on
# the deployment — not one per service, and no key id. From a k8s secret, never from this file
# with a real value in it.
#
# TWO LAYERS, AND THIS IS ONLY THE FIRST. The other is the gRPC port staying inside the
# cluster's internal network, unpublished. This key stops a process that reached the port; the
# network policy is what stops it reaching the port at all. Whoever removes one of them is
# removing half of the whole protection, not one measure among several (ADR 0025).
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT BUY, so nobody assumes more: it does not say WHICH service called, so an
# inter-service call cannot be attributed to a caller; anyone inside the cluster holding it can
# call anything; and because there is exactly one, ROTATING IT MEANS CHANGING IT EVERYWHERE AT
# ONCE — a coordinated deployment, not a per-service action (rule 8, invariant 6). A rolling
# restart with half the fleet on the new value is a fleet refusing half its own calls.
#
# REQUIRED IN EVERY ENVIRONMENT, DEV INCLUDED. A service without it refuses to start and names
# this variable. No dev exemption, unlike SESSION_SIGNING_KEYS: a missing signing key means this
# process cannot read a session — local and visible — while a missing caller key means a gRPC
# port that accepts whatever reaches it, and "worked fine locally" is how that reaches a cluster.
#
#   generate:  openssl rand -base64 32
#
GRPC_CALLER_KEY=<dien-vao-tu-k8s-secret>

# ---- required for every service EXCEPT platform --------------------------------

# Where the platform service answers ResolveHost — the RPC that maps an incoming Host to a
# commune. Every service edge resolves its commune through this address before any handler runs.
#
# NO DEFAULT, on purpose: guessing the address of the registry is a default on the isolation
# path (rule 1, forbidden #1). A service that needs it and does not have it refuses to start and
# says so by name, rather than answering 404 for every domain — which reads like a DNS problem
# and sends somebody looking in the wrong place for an afternoon.
#
# The platform service itself leaves this empty: it holds the registry and calls nobody.
# Cluster-internal address only. There is no mTLS and no per-service identity on this hop: what
# guards it is GRPC_CALLER_KEY below plus the port staying off the public network — both, not
# either.
PLATFORM_GRPC_ADDR=platform:9090

# ---- required for every service that guards a STAFF route ----------------------

# Where the identity service answers ResolveStaffPrincipal — the RPC that turns a staff member's
# session cookie into the principal every guard needs. Without it, a guarded route in documents,
# finance, comms or petitions answers 401 to everybody, including a member of staff holding a
# perfectly valid session.
#
# NO DEFAULT, exactly like PLATFORM_GRPC_ADDR and for the same reason. A guessed address does not
# fail closed in a way anyone can read: every staff request answers 503 while identity is healthy,
# which looks like an outage of the wrong service.
#
# Two services leave it empty: identity itself builds its principal from its own session registry,
# and platform holds the registry and calls nobody.
#
# CLUSTER-INTERNAL ADDRESS ONLY, and the stakes are higher here than on the platform hop: what
# travels on it is a WORKING SESSION TOKEN, and there is no TLS and no per-service identity on this
# boundary (ADR 0025). An address that leaves the cluster puts every staff session on the wire in
# the clear.
IDENTITY_GRPC_ADDR=identity:9090

# How long a deactivated commune keeps being served from cache. Default 30s, short on purpose.
TENANT_CACHE_TTL=

# ---- dangerous — must be off before production --------------------------------

# Disables authentication. Local development only; leave EMPTY unless you mean it.
#
# Reported as a warning at EVERY startup, and refused outright when ENV=prod: a flag switched on
# for one local afternoon is forgotten by the next morning (rule 8, invariant 7).
DANGEROUS_AUTH_BYPASS=

# ---- tests --------------------------------------------------------------------

# Integration tests are SKIPPED when this is unset, and `go test ./...` still passes. Point it
# at a test database only — each run creates its own schema and drops it afterwards.
VIGOV_TEST_DSN=
